{
 "date": "2026-10-11",
 "host": {
  "description": "Azure VM, AMD EPYC 7763 (virtualized), 8 vCPU = 4 cores x 2 threads, 31 GiB, Ubuntu 24.04, kernel 6.17.0-azure; data disk Premium SSD v2 32 GiB (16,000 IOPS, 400 MB/s, host caching none) with ext4; OS disk Standard SSD. Native processes only (no Docker).",
  "cpu": "AMD EPYC 7763",
  "vcpus": 8,
  "cores": 4
 },
 "build": {
  "ruvio_commit": "5520164",
  "redis": "Redis 8.10.2 built from source (jemalloc 5.3.0); redis-benchmark is the 8.10.2 binary from the same build."
 },
 "method": {
  "features": "60 commands from <code>scripts/redis_compare_features.py</code> run through <code>scripts/pinned_run.py</code>: <code>redis-benchmark</code> 8.10.2, 50 clients, pipeline 16, 64-byte values, five rounds with alternating order, median requests/s. Ruvio and Redis each run alone on cpu 0 (core 0, its hyper-thread idle), the load generator on cpus 2 and 4 (two other cores), one shard, <code>memory</code> durability.",
  "concurrency": "five alternating rounds, median req/s and p99 (<code>redis-benchmark --threads 2</code>, 64-byte values, 100,000 random keys). Single-shard rows: server on cpu 0, client on cpus 2 and 4. Ruvio 2- and 4-shard rows: shards on cpus 0,2 and 0,2,4,6 and the client on the hyper-thread siblings 1,3,5,7. Redis is always one process (it is single-threaded) on cpu 0. <code>always</code> rows: Ruvio <code>--durability always</code> against Redis <code>appendonly yes</code>, <code>appendfsync always</code>, both on the Premium SSD v2 data disk.",
  "memory": "a fresh server per structure, load pipelined (batches of 200, replies checked), RSS from <code>/proc/&lt;pid&gt;/status</code> after loading minus RSS before (&ldquo;growth&rdquo;), plus the total RSS after loading, divided by the number of keys for the per-key columns."
 },
 "notes": {
  "concurrency": "With pipelining and 200 clients Ruvio is 1.11\u20131.43\u00d7 Redis with the lower p99. <code>always</code> (fsync) durability is where Ruvio leads most: 2.67\u00d7 at 50 clients, 1.50\u00d7 at 200 clients and 2.33\u00d7 at pipeline 16. Two changes since the last run explain it: the WAL fills each preallocated chunk with zeros ahead of the write cursor on a helper thread (an <code>fdatasync</code> of written blocks cost 0.8 ms instead of 2.0 ms on this disk), and a sync worker lets the shard keep executing while the sync runs. The un-pipelined rows are client-bound: the load generator tops out near 110\u2013120k req/s on this host for both servers (identical GET numbers), so they show the p99 tail, not server capacity. More Ruvio shards lift pipelined SET from 1.14M to 1.33M req/s at 2 shards; at 4 shards the client, which shares cores with the shards, is the limit. With <code>always</code>, 2 shards and 200 clients at pipeline 16 Ruvio is at parity with Redis (1.00\u00d7): extra shards do not help there because disk flush latency, not shard CPU, is the limit.",
  "limits": "one VM, one CPU model (virtualized AMD EPYC 7763), native processes only, loopback, 64-byte values, short runs, five rounds. The data disk is network-attached (an <code>fdatasync</code> costs about 0.8 ms); a local NVMe disk would change every <code>always</code> row for both servers. Server and load generator share the VM, so absolute numbers describe this host; compare the two servers with each other only.",
  "memory": "Growth is Ruvio's RSS increase from loading against Redis's. Redis's idle RSS on this machine was 13.2 MiB (8.4 MiB on the previous one), which pre-faults pages and makes its growth look smaller, so the total RSS after loading is the steadier number: Ruvio is lower in all 12 rows (0.71\u20130.86\u00d7). By growth Ruvio is lower in 7 rows, equal in 1 and higher in 4 (lists of 20 elements, HyperLogLogs, 128-byte bitmaps, geo points). Ruvio's own growth values are unchanged from the previous run (within 0.2 MiB), so the difference comes from the Redis side."
 },
